WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — ai-reads-human (180s) Narrator Sean

1
00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.167
First, computers often swap data in text formats written for human eyes,

2
00:00:05.167 --> 00:00:07.638
forcing machines to spend extra work.

3
00:00:08.198 --> 00:00:12.420
Second, reading indented configuration text took over two thousand

4
00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:16.258
times more chip energy than reading compact machine numbers.

5
00:00:16.818 --> 00:00:20.862
Third, chopping structured text into AI word pieces burned

6
00:00:20.862 --> 00:00:24.628
twelve times more energy than parsing the text itself.

7
00:00:25.188 --> 00:00:32.186
Why do two servers sitting inches apart exchange data in spelled-out English punctuation? Over four decades, web

8
00:00:32.186 --> 00:00:39.808
specifications put human readability ahead of machine efficiency so engineers could inspect messages in text editors. When

9
00:00:39.808 --> 00:00:47.243
autonomous AI agents arrived, developers stacked millions of automated tool calls right on top of those chatty formats.

10
00:00:47.803 --> 00:00:54.621
Picture two factory robots turning every bolt measurement into a typed business letter, sealing the envelope, and

11
00:00:54.621 --> 00:00:59.870
hiring a clerk on the other side to read every digit back into memory. Worse yet, loose

12
00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:07.472
text rules smuggle silent bugs, like indented configuration parsers reading Norway's two-letter country code as a false value.

13
00:01:08.032 --> 00:01:14.099
To put exact physical units on this impedance tax, we built a reproducible corpus of twenty thousand

14
00:01:14.099 --> 00:01:21.197
structured records across twelve formats. Every parser and AI word-piece splitter ran five shuffled rounds inside its

15
00:01:21.197 --> 00:01:29.630
own dedicated worker process, recording the operating system's built-in per-process chip energy counter alongside whole-chip power sensors.

16
00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:37.073
The measured energy curve spans more than three orders of magnitude for the exact same underlying data. Fixed

17
00:01:37.073 --> 00:01:44.461
binary unpacking sits at the floor at two hundred eighty nanojoules per record, standard bracketed text climbs nearly

18
00:01:44.461 --> 00:01:53.554
eightfold, compiled indented text jumps two hundred forty-nine-fold, and pure Python indented text reaches five hundred seventy-six microjoules.

19
00:01:54.114 --> 00:02:03.409
Our three-dimensional funnel contrasts sprawling clouds of braces, quotation marks, and repeated field names against dense twenty-three-byte binary

20
00:02:03.409 --> 00:02:10.555
records backed by a shared word table. Simply adding two spaces of pretty indentation to structured text inflated

21
00:02:10.555 --> 00:02:17.511
AI word-splitting energy two and a half times to seventy-one microjoules per record just to scan blank spaces.

22
00:02:18.071 --> 00:02:26.109
Across all one hundred seventy measurement windows, chopping compact structured text into AI word pieces burned twenty-seven

23
00:02:26.109 --> 00:02:34.083
thousand six hundred seventy-nine nanojoules per record. That single pre-processing step spent twelve and a half times more

24
00:02:34.083 --> 00:02:41.084
CPU energy than parsing the text itself, and ninety-nine times more than reading binary, before any GPU ran.

25
00:02:41.644 --> 00:02:49.325
Scientific rigor means stating clearly what counters can and cannot prove. Our five-percent idle-drift check caught background

26
00:02:49.325 --> 00:02:56.701
co-tenant noise on one hundred nine of one hundred seventy whole-chip laptop windows, confirming why isolated per-process

27
00:02:56.701 --> 00:03:04.382
chip counters were essential, while external wall-meter power probes and GPU model inference energy remain explicitly planned.
